### fix FUTON_DBO_A_03

- section: DBO   tier: Advanced
- title: how many futons the available components can build
- why: Inner joins discard components without inventory, overstating capacity. Missing stock must contribute zero, and negative availability cannot imply negative production.
- request: How many of each finished futon can WH-MAIN build from its direct components, ignoring scrap, taking the smallest whole-number capacity across its components? Show NULL when no active bill of materials exists.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT p.ItemCode,
           p.ItemName,
           MIN(FLOOR(CASE
                         WHEN v.QuantityAvailable > 0 THEN v.QuantityAvailable
                         ELSE 0
                     END / b.Quantity)) AS Buildable
    FROM Items p
             JOIN ItemType t ON t.ItemTypeID = p.ItemTypeID
             LEFT JOIN BillOfMaterials b
                       ON b.ParentItemID = p.ItemID AND b.IsActive = 1
             LEFT JOIN Inventory v
                       ON v.ItemID = b.ComponentItemID
                           AND v.WarehouseID = (SELECT w.WarehouseID
                                                FROM Warehouse w
                                                WHERE w.WarehouseCode = 'WH-MAIN')
    WHERE t.TypeCode = 'FG'
    GROUP BY p.ItemID, p.ItemCode, p.ItemName
    ORDER BY Buildable DESC, p.ItemCode
    ;
    ```

### fix FUTON_DBO_A_01

- section: DBO   tier: Advanced
- title: the raw materials ten futons need
- why: A terminal component is not necessarily a raw material. Filter by RAW and report zero availability when inventory is absent; guard the BOM assumptions with the proposed checks.
- request: What raw materials do ten FG-FUT-003 futons require through every active BOM level, including each level's scrap allowance, and how much is available across all warehouses?
- sql:

    ```sql
    WITH Explosion AS (SELECT b.ComponentItemID,
                              CAST(10 * b.Quantity * (1 + b.ScrapRate / 100)
                                  AS DECIMAL(28, 8)) AS Quantity
                       FROM BillOfMaterials b
                                JOIN Items p ON p.ItemID = b.ParentItemID
                       WHERE p.ItemCode = 'FG-FUT-003'
                         AND b.IsActive = 1
                       UNION ALL
                       SELECT b.ComponentItemID,
                              CAST(e.Quantity * b.Quantity * (1 + b.ScrapRate / 100)
                                  AS DECIMAL(28, 8))
                       FROM Explosion e
                                JOIN BillOfMaterials b
                                     ON b.ParentItemID = e.ComponentItemID
                       WHERE b.IsActive = 1)
    SELECT i.ItemCode,
           i.ItemName,
           CAST(SUM(e.Quantity) AS DECIMAL(18, 4)) AS Quantity,
           u.UnitCode,
           COALESCE((SELECT SUM(v.QuantityAvailable)
                     FROM Inventory v
                     WHERE v.ItemID = i.ItemID), 0) AS Available
    FROM Explosion e
             JOIN Items i ON i.ItemID = e.ComponentItemID
             JOIN ItemType t ON t.ItemTypeID = i.ItemTypeID
             JOIN UnitOfMeasure u ON u.UnitID = i.UnitID
    WHERE t.TypeCode = 'RAW'
    GROUP BY i.ItemID, i.ItemCode, i.ItemName, u.UnitCode
    ORDER BY i.ItemCode
    OPTION (MAXRECURSION 0)
    ;
    ```

### fix FUTON_DBO_I_02

- section: DBO   tier: Intermediate
- title: each finished futon's direct material cost and margin
- why: Starting from BOM rows omits finished goods without active components. Include every finished good and distinguish a missing BOM from a calculated cost.
- request: What is each finished futon's direct material cost, including scrap, and what remains of its list price? Show NULL cost and margin when it has no active BOM.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT p.ItemCode,
           p.ItemName,
           CAST(SUM(b.Quantity * (1 + b.ScrapRate / 100) * c.StandardCost)
               AS DECIMAL(18, 4)) AS MaterialCost,
           p.ListPrice,
           CAST(p.ListPrice
                    - SUM(b.Quantity * (1 + b.ScrapRate / 100) * c.StandardCost)
               AS DECIMAL(18, 4)) AS Margin
    FROM Items p
             JOIN ItemType t ON t.ItemTypeID = p.ItemTypeID
             LEFT JOIN BillOfMaterials b
                       ON b.ParentItemID = p.ItemID AND b.IsActive = 1
             LEFT JOIN Items c ON c.ItemID = b.ComponentItemID
    WHERE t.TypeCode = 'FG'
    GROUP BY p.ItemID, p.ItemCode, p.ItemName, p.ListPrice
    ORDER BY p.ItemCode
    ;
    ```

### fix FUTON_DBO_I_04

- section: DBO   tier: Intermediate
- title: rejection percentage on incoming inspections
- why: A PO reference alone does not identify an incoming inspection. The denominator can also be zero; represent an undefined percentage as NULL.
- request: For each supplier with incoming inspections referencing its purchase orders, what percentage of the inspected quantity was rejected, worst first? Show NULL when the inspected total is zero.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT s.SupplierCode,
           s.SupplierName,
           SUM(q.QuantityInspected) AS Inspected,
           SUM(q.QuantityRejected) AS Rejected,
           CAST(SUM(q.QuantityRejected) * 100
                    / NULLIF(SUM(q.QuantityInspected), 0)
               AS DECIMAL(9, 2)) AS RejectedPercent
    FROM PurchaseOrder po
             JOIN Supplier s ON s.SupplierID = po.SupplierID
             JOIN QualityInspection q
                  ON q.ReferenceType = 'PO'
                      AND q.ReferenceNumber = po.PONumber
                      AND q.InspectionType = 'Incoming'
    GROUP BY s.SupplierID, s.SupplierCode, s.SupplierName
    ORDER BY RejectedPercent DESC, s.SupplierCode
    ;
    ```

### fix FUTON_DBO_I_03

- section: DBO   tier: Intermediate
- title: the five stocks with the lowest reorder ratios
- why: Inventory is unique by item and warehouse, so ItemCode alone cannot resolve ties. “Nearest” is also misleading for a ratio ordered from smallest upward.
- request: Which five item-and-warehouse stocks have the lowest available-quantity-to-reorder-point ratios, considering only positive reorder points? Break ties by item code and warehouse code.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT TOP (5) i.ItemCode,
                   i.ItemName,
                   w.WarehouseCode,
                   v.QuantityAvailable,
                   i.ReorderPoint,
                   CAST(v.QuantityAvailable / i.ReorderPoint AS DECIMAL(18, 2)) AS Ratio
    FROM Inventory v
             JOIN Items i ON i.ItemID = v.ItemID
             JOIN Warehouse w ON w.WarehouseID = v.WarehouseID
    WHERE i.ReorderPoint > 0
    ORDER BY v.QuantityAvailable / i.ReorderPoint, i.ItemCode, w.WarehouseCode
    ;
    ```

### fix FUTON_DBO_I_05

- section: DBO   tier: Intermediate
- title: orders and net amount for every sales channel
- why: The inner join excludes channels without orders, and equal totals have unstable ordering. Preserve channels and count matched orders.
- request: For every sales channel, how many orders and how much recorded net amount are there, largest amount first? Show zero for channels without orders and count orders whose net amount is NULL separately.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT ch.ChannelCode,
           ch.ChannelName,
           COUNT(so.SalesOrderID) AS Orders,
           COALESCE(SUM(so.NetAmount), 0) AS NetAmount,
           COUNT(so.SalesOrderID) - COUNT(so.NetAmount) AS UnknownNetAmounts
    FROM SalesChannel ch
             LEFT JOIN SalesOrder so ON so.SalesChannelID = ch.SalesChannelID
    GROUP BY ch.SalesChannelID, ch.ChannelCode, ch.ChannelName
    ORDER BY NetAmount DESC, ch.ChannelCode
    ;
    ```

### fix FUTON_DBO_B_05

- section: DBO   tier: Basic
- title: orders awaiting delivery
- why: The request leaves cancelled orders ambiguous, and SQL Server places missing delivery dates before known dates. Make the delivery queue and missing-date placement explicit.
- request: Which orders are awaiting delivery, excluding cancelled orders, earliest requested delivery first and undated orders last?
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT so.OrderNumber, c.CustomerName, so.Status,
           so.RequestedDeliveryDate, so.NetAmount
    FROM SalesOrder so
             JOIN Customer c ON c.CustomerID = so.CustomerID
    WHERE so.Status NOT IN ('Delivered', 'Cancelled')
    ORDER BY CASE WHEN so.RequestedDeliveryDate IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END,
             so.RequestedDeliveryDate, so.OrderNumber
    ;
    ```

### add FUTON_DBO_I_07

- section: DBO   tier: Intermediate
- title: BOM links that violate planning assumptions
- why: The DDL does not enforce positive quantities, known scrap rates, consistent units, unique active parent/component pairs, or raw materials being leaves. This check guards assumptions used by the existing counts, costs, and recursive quantities.
- request: Which active BOM links have nonpositive quantities, missing or negative scrap rates, units different from their component's stock unit, repeated parent/component pairs, or raw-material parents?
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT b.BOMID,
           p.ItemCode AS ParentCode,
           c.ItemCode AS ComponentCode,
           b.Quantity,
           b.ScrapRate,
           bu.UnitCode AS BOMUnit,
           cu.UnitCode AS StockUnit
    FROM BillOfMaterials b
             JOIN Items p ON p.ItemID = b.ParentItemID
             JOIN ItemType pt ON pt.ItemTypeID = p.ItemTypeID
             JOIN Items c ON c.ItemID = b.ComponentItemID
             JOIN UnitOfMeasure bu ON bu.UnitID = b.UnitID
             JOIN UnitOfMeasure cu ON cu.UnitID = c.UnitID
    WHERE b.IsActive = 1
      AND (b.Quantity <= 0
        OR b.ScrapRate IS NULL
        OR b.ScrapRate < 0
        OR b.UnitID <> c.UnitID
        OR pt.TypeCode = 'RAW'
        OR EXISTS (SELECT 1
                   FROM BillOfMaterials other
                   WHERE other.ParentItemID = b.ParentItemID
                     AND other.ComponentItemID = b.ComponentItemID
                     AND other.IsActive = 1
                     AND other.BOMID <> b.BOMID))
    ORDER BY p.ItemCode, c.ItemCode, b.BOMID
    ;
    ```

### add FUTON_DBO_A_05

- section: DBO   tier: Advanced
- title: items participating in BOM cycles
- why: CHK_BOM_NotSelf prevents only direct self-reference. A cycle check guards both existing recursive exercises before unrestricted recursion is used.
- request: Which items participate in a cycle of active BOM links?
- sql:

    ```sql
    WITH Walk AS (SELECT b.ParentItemID AS StartItemID,
                         b.ComponentItemID AS CurrentItemID,
                         CAST('/' + CAST(b.ParentItemID AS VARCHAR(11))
                                  + '/' + CAST(b.ComponentItemID AS VARCHAR(11))
                                  + '/' AS VARCHAR(MAX)) AS ItemPath,
                         0 AS IsCycle
                  FROM BillOfMaterials b
                  WHERE b.IsActive = 1
                  UNION ALL
                  SELECT w.StartItemID,
                         b.ComponentItemID,
                         CAST(w.ItemPath + CAST(b.ComponentItemID AS VARCHAR(11))
                                  + '/' AS VARCHAR(MAX)),
                         CASE WHEN CHARINDEX('/'
                                                 + CAST(b.ComponentItemID AS VARCHAR(11))
                                                 + '/', w.ItemPath) > 0
                                  THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
                  FROM Walk w
                           JOIN BillOfMaterials b
                                ON b.ParentItemID = w.CurrentItemID
                  WHERE b.IsActive = 1
                    AND w.IsCycle = 0)
    SELECT DISTINCT i.ItemCode, i.ItemName
    FROM Walk w
             JOIN Items i ON i.ItemID = w.StartItemID
    WHERE w.IsCycle = 1
      AND w.CurrentItemID = w.StartItemID
    ORDER BY i.ItemCode
    OPTION (MAXRECURSION 0)
    ;
    ```

### add FUTON_DBO_B_06

- section: DBO   tier: Basic
- title: the open production schedule
- why: Production orders and their nullable work-center assignment are uncovered. An outer join preserves work awaiting assignment.
- request: Show planned, released, and in-progress production orders with their item, warehouse, work center, priority, and completion deadline; put known deadlines first, earliest first.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT po.WorkOrderNumber, i.ItemCode, w.WarehouseCode,
           wc.WorkCenterCode, po.Status, po.Priority, po.PlannedCompletionDate
    FROM ProductionOrder po
             JOIN Items i ON i.ItemID = po.ItemID
             JOIN Warehouse w ON w.WarehouseID = po.WarehouseID
             LEFT JOIN WorkCenter wc ON wc.WorkCenterID = po.WorkCenterID
    WHERE po.Status IN ('Planned', 'Released', 'InProgress')
    ORDER BY CASE WHEN po.PlannedCompletionDate IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END,
             po.PlannedCompletionDate,
             CASE WHEN po.Priority IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END,
             po.Priority, po.WorkOrderNumber
    ;
    ```

### add FUTON_DBO_I_08

- section: DBO   tier: Intermediate
- title: production materials still awaiting issue
- why: ProductionOrderMaterial supplies a separate, uncovered requirements path. Compare each order's remaining material requirement with its warehouse stock without multiplying inventory rows.
- request: For open production orders, show each material still awaiting issue and its availability in the order's warehouse. Treat missing inventory as zero and report materials with unknown issued quantities separately.
- sql:

    ```sql
    WITH Materials AS (SELECT m.ProductionOrderID,
                              m.ItemID,
                              SUM(m.RequiredQuantity - m.IssuedQuantity) AS Remaining,
                              SUM(CASE WHEN m.IssuedQuantity IS NULL
                                           THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS UnknownIssued
                       FROM ProductionOrderMaterial m
                       GROUP BY m.ProductionOrderID, m.ItemID)
    SELECT po.WorkOrderNumber,
           i.ItemCode,
           w.WarehouseCode,
           CASE WHEN m.UnknownIssued = 0 THEN m.Remaining END AS Remaining,
           COALESCE(v.QuantityAvailable, 0) AS Available,
           m.UnknownIssued
    FROM Materials m
             JOIN ProductionOrder po ON po.ProductionOrderID = m.ProductionOrderID
             JOIN Items i ON i.ItemID = m.ItemID
             JOIN Warehouse w ON w.WarehouseID = po.WarehouseID
             LEFT JOIN Inventory v
                       ON v.ItemID = m.ItemID AND v.WarehouseID = po.WarehouseID
    WHERE po.Status IN ('Planned', 'Released', 'InProgress')
      AND (m.Remaining > 0 OR m.UnknownIssued > 0)
    ORDER BY po.WorkOrderNumber, i.ItemCode
    ;
    ```

### add FUTON_DBO_I_09

- section: DBO   tier: Intermediate
- title: purchase lines still awaiting receipt
- why: PurchaseOrderDetail and partial receipts are uncovered. Nullable QuantityReceived needs an explicit unknown state, and LineNumber is not a declared unique key.
- request: Which noncancelled purchase-order lines have quantities still outstanding or an unknown received quantity? Show the supplier, receiving warehouse, and expected delivery date.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT po.PONumber,
           d.LineNumber,
           d.PODetailID,
           s.SupplierCode,
           w.WarehouseCode,
           i.ItemCode,
           d.Quantity,
           d.QuantityReceived,
           d.Quantity - d.QuantityReceived AS Outstanding,
           po.ExpectedDeliveryDate
    FROM PurchaseOrder po
             JOIN PurchaseOrderDetail d ON d.PurchaseOrderID = po.PurchaseOrderID
             JOIN Supplier s ON s.SupplierID = po.SupplierID
             JOIN Warehouse w ON w.WarehouseID = po.WarehouseID
             JOIN Items i ON i.ItemID = d.ItemID
    WHERE po.Status <> 'Cancelled'
      AND (d.QuantityReceived < d.Quantity OR d.QuantityReceived IS NULL)
    ORDER BY CASE WHEN po.ExpectedDeliveryDate IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END,
             po.ExpectedDeliveryDate, po.PONumber, d.LineNumber, d.PODetailID
    ;
    ```

### add FUTON_DBO_I_10

- section: DBO   tier: Intermediate
- title: returned quantities and refunds by reason and item
- why: Returns, reasons, and the link back to an original sales line are uncovered. Summing detail refunds avoids repeating a header refund for every returned item.
- request: For received or refunded returns, show returned quantity and line refunds by reason and item, with the number of original sales lines affected.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT rr.ReasonCode,
           rr.ReasonDescription,
           i.ItemCode,
           i.ItemName,
           COUNT(DISTINCT d.SODetailID) AS SalesLines,
           SUM(d.QuantityReturned) AS QuantityReturned,
           SUM(d.RefundAmount) AS RefundAmount
    FROM SalesReturn r
             JOIN ReturnReason rr ON rr.ReturnReasonID = r.ReturnReasonID
             JOIN SalesReturnDetail d ON d.ReturnID = r.ReturnID
             JOIN Items i ON i.ItemID = d.ItemID
    WHERE r.Status IN ('Received', 'Refunded')
    GROUP BY rr.ReturnReasonID, rr.ReasonCode, rr.ReasonDescription,
             i.ItemID, i.ItemCode, i.ItemName
    ORDER BY RefundAmount DESC, rr.ReasonCode, i.ItemCode
    ;
    ```

### add FUTON_DBO_B_07

- section: DBO   tier: Basic
- title: accepted quotations and their resulting orders
- why: This covers the nullable quotation-to-order foreign key and optional sales representative and territory assignments.
- request: Show accepted quotations, their customers, assigned representatives and representatives' territories, and resulting order numbers; include quotations still awaiting conversion.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT q.QuoteNumber,
           c.CustomerCode,
           sr.EmployeeCode,
           st.TerritoryCode,
           q.QuoteDate,
           so.OrderNumber,
           so.OrderDate
    FROM SalesQuote q
             JOIN Customer c ON c.CustomerID = q.CustomerID
             LEFT JOIN SalesRep sr ON sr.SalesRepID = q.SalesRepID
             LEFT JOIN SalesTerritory st ON st.TerritoryID = sr.TerritoryID
             LEFT JOIN SalesOrder so ON so.SalesOrderID = q.ConvertedToOrderID
    WHERE q.Status = 'Accepted'
    ORDER BY q.QuoteDate, q.QuoteNumber
    ;
    ```

### add FUTON_DBO_B_08

- section: DBO   tier: Basic
- title: an item's monthly inventory transaction history
- why: InventoryTransaction and TransactionType are uncovered. A half-open date range correctly includes all DATETIME2 values in the month, with the primary key resolving equal timestamps.
- request: Show the November 2026 inventory transactions for RM-FILL-001, with warehouse, transaction type, signed quantity, recorded unit cost, and reference, in chronological order.
- sql:

    ```sql
    SELECT it.TransactionID,
           it.TransactionDate,
           w.WarehouseCode,
           tt.TypeCode,
           it.Quantity,
           it.UnitCost,
           it.ReferenceType,
           it.ReferenceNumber
    FROM InventoryTransaction it
             JOIN Items i ON i.ItemID = it.ItemID
             JOIN Warehouse w ON w.WarehouseID = it.WarehouseID
             JOIN TransactionType tt ON tt.TransactionTypeID = it.TransactionTypeID
    WHERE i.ItemCode = 'RM-FILL-001'
      AND it.TransactionDate >= '20261101'
      AND it.TransactionDate < '20261201'
    ORDER BY it.TransactionDate, it.TransactionID
    ;
    ```
